---
// About: the same intro as the home, so the label, the title and the map sit at the same place on both pages, then
// one part per island of the map. Each part's heading stays in view under the intro text while its text, under the
// map, is read. The map's orange dots link to the parts.
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Intro from '../components/Intro.astro';
import JsonLd from '../components/JsonLd.astro';

// JSON-LD structured data for Person
const personSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "Person",
  "name": "Josselin Perrus",
  "url": "https://meaningfool.net/about/",
  "jobTitle": "Product manager",
  "sameAs": [
    "https://github.com/meaningfool",
    "https://twitter.com/nonils",
    "https://www.linkedin.com/in/josselinperrus/"
  ]
};

// The boundary model Skills, in the owner's public skills repository.
const boundaryModelSkills = 'https://github.com/meaningfool/skills/tree/main/skills/boundary-model';
---

<Layout title="About | meaningfool" description="About Josselin Perrus - Product manager based in Paris">
	<JsonLd data={personSchema} />
	<main id="main" class="about">
		<Intro label="About" links={{ 'customer-success': '#customer-success', product: '#product', 'ai-agents': '#ai-agents' }}>
			<Fragment slot="title">Customer success,<br />Product,<br />AI agents<span>.</span></Fragment>
			I'm Josselin Perrus, product manager turned builder. I started in customer-facing roles, spent seven years building the product at Linkurious (2026 exit), and the last three years building with AI.
		</Intro>

		<div class="parts">
			<section class="part" id="customer-success" aria-labelledby="customer-success-name">
				<div class="tag">
					<h2 id="customer-success-name">Customer Success</h2>
					<p>Identify the value</p>
				</div>
				<div class="text">
					<p>I started in client-facing positions. Talking to customers and making them successful is still the highlight of my day. It's also where I learnt to listen and be pragmatic.</p>
					<p>Listen, because when you have little leverage on the product, that's your only chance to narrow the problem enough that you may be able to find a solution.</p>
					<p>Pragmatic, because a now-solution beats everyday a beautiful solution.</p>
				</div>
			</section>

			<section class="part" id="product" aria-labelledby="product-name">
				<div class="tag">
					<h2 id="product-name">Product</h2>
					<p>Decide what to build</p>
				</div>
				<div class="text">
					<p>Linkurious grew from €1K / year / customer to over €100K / year / customer. Self-financed. As the Founding PM, I had to identify the highest leverage bets, and build alignment with the cofounders. And push back on the rest.</p>
					<p>I build focus and clarity in fluid environments on a deep understanding of customers (120 interviews in the first 6 months at Linkurious), their problems, and how they create opportunities to meet the company strategy.</p>
				</div>
			</section>

			<section class="part" id="ai-agents" aria-labelledby="ai-agents-name">
				<div class="tag">
					<h2 id="ai-agents-name">AI Agents</h2>
					<p>Software factory</p>
				</div>
				<div class="text">
					<p>My very first AI project was Dec. 2023: building a knowledge graph from a sum of articles using structured extraction. Later came a data lineage tool, a report on agent frameworks and 2 voice agent projects.</p>
					<p>I build them end to end with coding agents, from the infrastructure to the prompts, from backend/frontend to benchmarks and evals.</p>
					<p>I <em>compound</em> building my own set of tools such as my <em>boundary model</em> Skills (<a href={boundaryModelSkills}>GitHub</a>).</p>
				</div>
			</section>
		</div>
	</main>
</Layout>

<style>
	.about {
		padding-bottom: 24px;
	}

	/* The parts sit on the intro's two columns: the heading under the intro text, the text under the map. */
	.part {
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: 500px minmax(480px, 636px);
		justify-content: space-between;
		gap: 12px 56px;
		padding: 32px 0 44px;
		border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
		scroll-margin-top: 24px;
	}

	/* The heading stays in view while its part is read. */
	.tag {
		position: sticky;
		top: 32px;
		align-self: start;
		display: grid;
		gap: 6px;
	}

	/* The orange dot, then the name. */
	h2 {
		display: flex;
		align-items: center;
		gap: 10px;
		margin: 0;
		font-weight: 400;
		font-size: 18px;
		line-height: 1.6;
		letter-spacing: -0.04em;
	}

	h2::before {
		content: '';
		flex: none;
		width: 9px;
		height: 9px;
		border-radius: 50%;
		background: var(--accent);
	}

	/* The map's label for the part, in grey capitals. */
	.tag p {
		font-size: 10px;
		line-height: 1.6;
		letter-spacing: 0.08em;
		text-transform: uppercase;
		color: var(--muted);
	}

	/* The article's serif and size. */
	.text {
		display: grid;
		gap: 20px;
		font-family: var(--serif);
		font-size: 20px;
		line-height: 32px;
	}

	.text a {
		color: inherit;
		text-decoration: underline;
		text-decoration-color: var(--accent);
		text-underline-position: auto;
		text-underline-offset: 4px;
	}

	/* The map's dots link to the parts: scroll there smoothly, unless motion is to be reduced. */
	@media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
		:global(:root:has(.about .parts)) {
			scroll-behavior: smooth;
		}
	}

	/* Below 1120px, as the intro, one column, up to 720px. */
	@media (max-width: 1119px) {
		.part {
			grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 720px);
			justify-content: start;
		}

		.tag {
			position: static;
		}
	}

	@media (max-width: 900px) {
		.text {
			font-size: 18px;
			line-height: 29px;
		}
	}
</style>
